WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.000
-Kendall, thank you so much
for coming back to our show.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.701
-Yeah, thanks for having me.
-You look gorgeous, oh, my gosh.

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:06.334
-This is so nice to be back.

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:08.000
-Yeah, feels good
with an audience, right?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.033
-Yeah, it does.

00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:11.400
-I want to get into
some cool stuff here,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.534
but I want to say first,
your sister Kylie

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:15.367
is expecting her second child.

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:16.901
-She is, she is.
It's really exciting.

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:18.067
-Congratulations.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:20.501
-Thank you.
-How did you -- How did you --

00:00:20.501 --> 00:00:23.501
How'd you hear the news?
-She called me,

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:25.434
and I answered the phone,
and she just had

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:27.868
her little sonogram -- like,
a picture of her sonogram.

00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:30.534
And I was like, "Oh, my God."
I wasn't really shocked

00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:33.334
'cause I, like, felt like
it could happen someday soon,

00:00:33.334 --> 00:00:36.601
but I was excited.
I was very excited, and --

00:00:36.601 --> 00:00:38.033
It's just a blessing,
it's amazing.

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:40.667
-Are you a good babysitter?
-I'm all right.

00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:43.467
I always want to be, like,
the cool aunt and, like, liked,

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:45.501
I guess, by all of them.
-Yes! Yeah, me too.

00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:47.734
-There is 18 of them now,
going on 20.

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:49.434
There's two on the way.
-Is that right?

00:00:49.434 --> 00:00:52.067
-So, yeah, my brother, Burt,
has one on the way,

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:54.200
and then, my sister, obviously,
just announced hers, so...

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.534
-Oh, my gosh.
So you're pretty used to it.

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:58.167
-I have a lot of them.
But it's fun.

00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:01.634
It's really fun.
I try and be chill.

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:04.067
-Yeah. Does your boyfriend
help out at all?

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:05.267
-He loves them.

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.267
Him and Stormi
have an amazing relationship.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.667
She has
the biggest crush on him,

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:10.300
and I'm, like,
jealous sometimes.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.701
I'm like, "Stop."
-Yeah, you're like,

00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:12.901
"Come on," yeah.
-I'm like, "Can you guys not?"

00:01:12.901 --> 00:01:14.634
-"I'm the cool one," yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:14.634 --> 00:01:17.033
-Devin Booker
is on the Phoenix Suns.

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:18.834
He's amazing.
Just won a gold medal.

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:20.601
-He did, yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:23.534
It's awesome.

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:25.767
-I know that we talked about it.
You were very sporty

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:27.501
when you were growing up.
You played basketball.

00:01:27.501 --> 00:01:29.200
-I did in ele--
Er, yeah,

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.601
middle school
I played basketball.

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:33.234
-Do you guys ever have a little
one-on-one competition?

00:01:33.234 --> 00:01:37.200
-We do sometimes. He --
We actually were just playing.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.834
We have, like -- our friends
had, like, a pool setup.

00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:41.234
You know, the hoops
that are in the pool?

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:42.934
-Oh, there you go.
-And so we were playing

00:01:42.934 --> 00:01:45.534
in the pool, and he just, like,
took it way too seriously --

00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:47.234
as did I, by the way.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.234 --> 00:01:50.634
And I was like kind of --
-Dunking on you and stuff?

00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:52.400
-I had the ball,
and he was guarding me,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.701
and it was a whole thing.
He actually ended up

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:56.567
cutting my hand open a little
with his nail.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:57.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.067
'Cause he took it
a little too seriously.

00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:01.267
It's pretty much healed now,
so you can't really see it,

00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:04.501
but it was a big gash, and it
bothered me for like two weeks.

00:02:04.501 --> 00:02:06.367
-Oh, my gosh, yeah.
Plus, you probably

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:08.701
had pool hands, too,
for being in there, yeah.

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:10.434
I saw you last night
at the Met Gala.

00:02:10.434 --> 00:02:12.601
-Yeah.
-And you looked stunning.

00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:14.267
-Thank you.
-Look at this.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.968
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:16.968 --> 00:02:19.601
-I mean, holy moly.
-Thank you.

00:02:19.601 --> 00:02:22.100
[ Cheers and applause
continues ]

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.901
-How long does it take you
to get ready for these events?

00:02:25.901 --> 00:02:28.234
-That took
probably like two hours.

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:29.901
-Yeah.
-Which --

00:02:29.901 --> 00:02:31.400
And it was, like, calm.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.801
-Like, I wasn't --
Usually, I feel like

00:02:32.801 --> 00:02:34.834
I'm a little more nervous...
-Yeah.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:36.868
-...most years,
and I was actually really just,

00:02:36.868 --> 00:02:38.701
like, calm.
I told myself before, I'm like,

00:02:38.701 --> 00:02:41.367
"Why stress yourself out
with unnecessary nerves.

00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:44.534
Just, like, be chill."
-I felt safe with all the COVID.

00:02:44.534 --> 00:02:45.968
I think they did a good job
with it.

00:02:45.968 --> 00:02:47.467
-Yeah, I felt like they did a
really good job with everything.

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.834
You were the first person I saw
when I walked in with my mom.

00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:51.501
-It's almost like I greeted
everyone on their way in.

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:52.701
-Well, by the way...
-They were like,

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:54.434
"Get to your table, Mr. Fallon."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.434 --> 00:02:56.100
-That's exactly
what my mom does.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.234
She'll stand at the top --
every year, she stands

00:02:58.234 --> 00:03:00.100
at the top of the carpet,
and security's like,

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.501
"Come on, ma'am. You have
to kind of go inside now.

00:03:02.501 --> 00:03:04.000
You can't just stand here
and wait around."

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.567
And she's like, "Absolutely not.

00:03:05.567 --> 00:03:07.667
I'm standing here and watching
all my daughters walk in."

00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:10.501
And she's just so cute,
and she takes videos of us.

00:03:10.501 --> 00:03:13.133
The smile on her face
is just everything.

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:14.467
-She's the greatest.
-She's the best.

00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:16.567
-Yeah, I loved seeing her,
as well.

00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:18.868
You're stepping into a different
role in the fashion world.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:23.033
You're the creative director
of this brand, Forward.

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:25.234
How do you --
When did you start that,

00:03:25.234 --> 00:03:27.234
and what does that entail?
What is that?

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:31.067
-So, Forward is a luxury
online retailer, so they --

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:34.667
Yeah, they sell a bunch of,
like, luxury brands online,

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:37.901
and it's really cool.
I think, for a long time,

00:03:37.901 --> 00:03:40.701
I was obviously in one space
of the fashion industry,

00:03:40.701 --> 00:03:43.767
and so it's cool
to kind of take a role

00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:45.767
in a more creative way,
personally.

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:48.334
So it's cool.
-Like, you do, like, a --

00:03:48.334 --> 00:03:51.601
like, choosing what goes --
-Yeah, so I get to choose, like,

00:03:51.601 --> 00:03:55.667
what is featured on the website,
and I actually had my first day

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:58.000
on the job the other day
here in New York,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.501
and I got to, like,
go around to a couple designers

00:04:00.501 --> 00:04:01.834
and pick things for the website,

00:04:01.834 --> 00:04:03.133
so it's actually
really, really fun.

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:05.334
I get to, you know,
shop a little, so...

00:04:05.334 --> 00:04:07.167
-You get to influence
a lot of people.

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:10.868
There was a giant headline
because you had

00:04:10.868 --> 00:04:13.200
the fashion world
going crazy because --

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.801
-Oh, no.
-Oh, very controversial.

00:04:15.801 --> 00:04:17.968
You wore Crocs.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:04:17.968 --> 00:04:20.334
-You wore Crocs.
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:25.400
-I -- [ Laughs ]
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.100
-Now the audience is on
your side here, but what --

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.467
-Yeah, yeah. Right.
-But I've never worn Crocs.

00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:32.701
Questlove swears by Crocs.
-They're the best.

00:04:32.701 --> 00:04:35.901
-Questlove is always, like,
"Dude, I lived long enough,

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:37.901
tried every sneaker,
shoe there is.

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:40.334
Crocs is my jam."
-Are you wearing it right now?

00:04:40.334 --> 00:04:42.801
-Yes, I am.
-Nice. Oh, I'm jealous.

00:04:42.801 --> 00:04:44.067
[ Cheers and applause ]
I'm jealous.

00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:46.701
-You're jealous? Yeah?
Are they that comfortable?

00:04:46.701 --> 00:04:48.133
I don't know what --
-They are.

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:51.234
I actually -- I saw those, like,
just on some, like,

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:53.434
Instagram inspiration, like,
fashion page,

00:04:53.434 --> 00:04:54.801
and I was like, "Wait.
These are actually cute,"

00:04:54.801 --> 00:04:57.167
so I went out of my way
to buy those specific Crocs

00:04:57.167 --> 00:05:01.667
but was not expecting to be
photographed that day, clearly.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:03.367
I'm really, really comfortable.

00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:04.634
-Yeah, that's a comfy outfit.
Yeah.

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:06.501
-They're great.
I am not ashamed whatsoever.

00:05:06.501 --> 00:05:07.968
-So, something like this
could maybe end up

00:05:07.968 --> 00:05:09.534
on Forward if you feel.
-Yeah.

00:05:09.534 --> 00:05:10.934
-Okay, yeah.
-Yeah, as Forward's

00:05:10.934 --> 00:05:12.767
creative director, I approve.
-Right?

00:05:12.767 --> 00:05:14.434
-I approve.
-I will tell you what

00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:16.534
I approve of, and that is...
-Oh, my gosh.

00:05:16.534 --> 00:05:19.367
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Whoo, doggie!

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:21.434
-Yes.
-Let me tell you something.

00:05:21.434 --> 00:05:23.267
It's delicious.
-Thank you.

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:25.200
-What made you get
into the spirits business?

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.334
-So, actually, my parents
were longtime lovers.

00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:31.100
I grew up hearing about it
around the house all the time.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:34.734
And so, kind as I've got older
and, you know, drank,

00:05:34.734 --> 00:05:37.868
I was always a tequila lover.
It's always my go-to drink,

00:05:37.868 --> 00:05:40.367
and me and my friends,
it's all we drink.

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:43.734
And, you know, I kind of --
was kind of the friend

00:05:43.734 --> 00:05:46.167
that was always
trying to find new tequilas,

00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:47.634
tequilas that not everyone
might know

00:05:47.634 --> 00:05:50.834
and just loved always
experimenting with it.

00:05:50.834 --> 00:05:52.601
You know, I kind of got
to a place where,

00:05:52.601 --> 00:05:53.934
although I found
a lot of tequilas

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:56.167
that I really, really loved,
I didn't really see one

00:05:56.167 --> 00:05:58.133
for my generation,
something that was, like,

00:05:58.133 --> 00:06:00.467
approachable
and aesthetically pleasing

00:06:00.467 --> 00:06:03.000
and socially aware
and gender neutral,

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.367
and there's, like,
so many things that I just

00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:06.334
didn't really see
for my generation.

00:06:06.334 --> 00:06:07.767
And so when I kind of
dove into it

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.601
and did a little bit
of research,

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:10.934
I found that
it was predominantly

00:06:10.934 --> 00:06:13.167
a male-dominated space,
and that kind of,

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:16.267
coming from a really strong
female family,

00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:18.367
it just drove me even harder
into the space

00:06:18.367 --> 00:06:20.567
and made me really excited
to get into it.

00:06:20.567 --> 00:06:23.501
And then, even diving
even further into it,

00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:28.067
I found that, you know,
not a lot of brands that I saw

00:06:28.067 --> 00:06:30.367
were as, you know,
friendly to the planet

00:06:30.367 --> 00:06:32.868
as they could possibly be,
so I took that upon myself

00:06:32.868 --> 00:06:36.701
to make that a really big part
of us as a brand.

00:06:36.701 --> 00:06:38.000
-Good for you.
-So we've actually partnered

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.267
with 1% for the Planet
to donate 1% of our revenue

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.567
to planet-saving initiatives,
and then, along with that --

00:06:43.567 --> 00:06:46.868
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah, it's cool.

00:06:46.868 --> 00:06:48.701
Along with that --
Sorry, this is a mouthful.

00:06:48.701 --> 00:06:50.067
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah, go for it.

00:06:50.067 --> 00:06:52.534
I like it.
-We actually --

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:55.133
at our distillery, which I was
just at the other day,

00:06:55.133 --> 00:06:58.467
we found a way to take
the agave waste

00:06:58.467 --> 00:07:01.100
and the agave fibers
and the water waste

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.534
and build the sustainable brick
that we are actually donating

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:06.267
back to the community
of Jalisco,

00:07:06.267 --> 00:07:09.367
so it's,
along with saving the planet,

00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:12.434
it's important to be friendly
to the community, as well.

00:07:12.434 --> 00:07:13.567
-I thought you were gonna say
you're gonna turn them

00:07:13.567 --> 00:07:15.534
into Crocs.
-No, no.

00:07:15.534 --> 00:07:17.834
We're donating them
and building homes for people

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:19.067
that need homes,
and there's half-built hospital.

00:07:19.067 --> 00:07:20.834
-Actual bricks?
-Yeah, but they're all

00:07:20.834 --> 00:07:22.901
sustainable.
They're made from the waste

00:07:22.901 --> 00:07:25.234
that comes from making tequila.
-Congrats, buddy.

00:07:25.234 --> 00:07:27.968
818 right here.
-Yeah. Try it out.

